# Malformed traffic. Hand built -- the capture is a clean link coming up.
#
# A bench analyzer meets all of these. Each must produce an error field and
# stop, never a confident wrong decode, and never a read past the phase.
#
# 1. An opcode Table 2 does not define. 0Ch sits in the gap between the flash
#    opcodes and the channel-independent ones.
CMD 0C 00

# 2. A defined opcode whose packet shape has not been transcribed yet.
#    PUT_PC is real (Table 2, p.25) but its header needs the cycle-type
#    layouts, which are stage D. The decoder must say so rather than guess a
#    length.
CMD 00 00

# 3. A good GET_CONFIGURATION command with one bit flipped in the CRC byte.
#    C9h where the capture has C8h.
CMD 21 00 20 C9
TAR
RSP 08 00 0B 00 00 0F 01 91

# 4. Chip select deasserting mid-header: the address field is one byte short
#    and there is no CRC at all.
CMD 21 00

# 5. A configuration address with the top four bits set. §3.7 p.38 says the
#    controller must drive them to zero and the target should ignore them --
#    so this transaction WORKS, reaches the Channel 1 register, and is still a
#    controller violating the specification. The decoder must say so rather
#    than mask the address off and report it as plain 0020h. Nothing else on
#    the bus will flag it.
CMD 21 F0 20 DC
TAR
RSP 08 00 0B 00 00 0F 01 91

# 6. A configuration address that is not DWord aligned. Bits [1:0] are
#    described as hard-wired to 00, so seeing 22h is an anomaly.
CMD 21 00 22 C6
TAR
RSP 08 00 00 00 00 0F 01 2E

# 7. A well formed address in a reserved range. 050h-7FFh is Reserved in
#    Table 21, so there is no register here to name bits in.
CMD 21 00 50 9F
TAR
RSP 08 00 00 00 00 0F 01 2E
